comment.body:
> I'm hoping there is some existing language we can reference from the URI spec to not have to repeat everything here. Basically the client should try to produce as "clean" or "normal" of a URL as possible, the AS is only expected to do strict string comparison.

@aaronpk - I updated it accordingly. Should be good enough from what I can tell.
I am still not sure about the percent-encoding. We might need to think about security implications. Let's continue discussions, but that can be separate from this PR.

@rcbart 

Regarding question 1: Section 13.15 does not replace or override section 13.10 so my assumption is that section 13.10 applies in the context of 13.15 as well. Given that so much of the policy here is deployment specific, my recommendation when it comes to guidance is that the deployments draft a specific document that describes how policy and rules for transaction tokens work within their environment.

The same principle applies to question 2. A deployment could most definitely "fail closed". I don't believe there is an "intended posture" as again, what matters is what's required for the deployment use cases. I would expect that a deployment in a regulated domain may have a different set of policy and rules than a deployment in say a publishing domain.

