ID-JAG encodes “which OAuth client may redeem this grant” rather than “which delegated actor may act.” Those are practically related but semantically different.
may_act would have modeled the delegation more cleanly, while client_id optimizes for existing OAuth AS implementation and client policy machinery.

Why weren't RFC 8693 delegation semantics not used? e.g:

- Name the approved delegated party using may_act, which could contain either a client_id (+ optional iss when not using CIMD), or a cnf claim referencing a public key the client controls, such as dpop key.
```
"may_act": {
  "client_id": "ras-client-id",
  "iss": "https://ras.example.com"
}
```

```
"may_act": {
  "cnf": {
    "jkt": "..."
  }
}
```

- Using cnf enables clients to exchange the JAG at the RAS while proving legitimate sender constraining but without having to be its client.

